{"subjectId":"sql","id":"logical-order","title":"Logical Order of Query Execution","group":"Querying Basics","order":17,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">You <em>write</em> <code>SELECT</code> first, but the database <em>evaluates</em> it almost last. This single fact explains most “why can't I use that here?” errors.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\">FROM / JOIN</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">WHERE</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">GROUP BY</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">HAVING</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">SELECT</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">DISTINCT</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">ORDER BY</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">LIMIT</div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">FROM + JOINs</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Build the working set of rows from the tables.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">WHERE</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Keep rows whose condition is true. Aggregates don't exist yet.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">GROUP BY</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Collapse rows into one row per group.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">HAVING</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Keep groups whose condition is true — aggregates are available now.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">5</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">SELECT</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Compute output expressions, window functions and aliases.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">6</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">DISTINCT</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Remove duplicate output rows.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">7</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">ORDER BY</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Sort — can use aliases, since <code>SELECT</code> already ran.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">8</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">LIMIT / OFFSET</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Cut the sorted result.</div></div></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bulb\"></i>Consequences</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You try</th><th>Result</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>WHERE monthly &gt; 9000</code> (alias)</td><td>Error in standard SQL</td><td><code>WHERE</code> runs before <code>SELECT</code> creates the alias</td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code></td><td>Error everywhere</td><td>Aggregates don't exist before <code>GROUP BY</code> — use <code>HAVING</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORDER BY monthly</code></td><td>Works</td><td><code>ORDER BY</code> runs after <code>SELECT</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (…) = 1</code></td><td>Error</td><td>Window functions are computed in <code>SELECT</code> — filter in an outer query / CTE</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS headcount\nFROM employees\nWHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2\nGROUP BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>misuse of aggregate: COUNT()</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*) AS headcount\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL           -- row filter: before grouping\nGROUP BY dept_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2                 -- group filter: after grouping\nORDER BY headcount DESC;            -- alias is fine here</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | headcount\n--------+----------\n1       | 4\n2       | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- to filter on a computed column, compute it in a subquery / CTE first\nSELECT name, monthly\nFROM (SELECT name, salary / 12 AS monthly FROM employees) AS t\nWHERE monthly &gt; 9000\nORDER BY monthly DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | monthly\n------+--------\nAsha  | 12500\nRavi  | 10000\nPriya | 9166</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>This is the <strong>logical</strong> order — it defines what a query means. The optimizer is free to execute it physically in any order that gives the same result (for example, pushing a filter into an index lookup).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the order of execution of a SQL query?</summary><div><p><code>FROM</code>/<code>JOIN</code> → <code>WHERE</code> → <code>GROUP BY</code> → <code>HAVING</code> → <code>SELECT</code> (incl. window functions) → <code>DISTINCT</code> → <code>ORDER BY</code> → <code>LIMIT</code>/<code>OFFSET</code>. That is why <code>WHERE</code> can't see aggregates or select-list aliases, and <code>ORDER BY</code> can.</p></div></details></section>"}